    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/a4b91e5c364411bb1b68f49faa082a4f57da926b2b3f31b76268b2646a189b93.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Use the unit-spaced tick marks to locate the listed points. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=- \\frac{13}{2}$\n- **II.** $x=-5$\n- **III.** $x=- \\frac{7}{2}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{13}{2}.$ Because there is a jump at $x=- \\frac{13}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{13}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-5.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-5$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{7}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=- \\frac{7}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{7}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\".",
