      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{3}{2}.$ Because there is a jump at $x=- \\frac{3}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{3}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\".",
